From 5ebe92efba0cf9092d6fb76be4a28fb987702557 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sim <sim@local>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 19:25:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] Raise parallel connection cap to 256 with byte-accurate
 window budget

Config chain: MAX_PARALLEL_CONNECTION_COUNT_NATIVE_MEMORY=256 remains the
single source of truth (read clamp, both write clamps); the settings slider
max becomes tier-aware via MemoryBudget.maxConnectionsForWindowBytes so the
UI never offers more than the engine honours (2GB @ 1MB chunks -> 174,
@ 512KB -> 256 capped, 3GB+ -> 256).

Memory: prefetchDepthBytes replaces the MB-based depth on the native tier.
The old formula rounded sub-MB chunks up to 1 MB and its coerceIn(conns+1,
...) floor silently oversold memory once conns+1 chunks no longer fit the
budget - 256 x 1MB needs 257MB against the 175MB budget of a 2GB device.
The byte-accurate variant never exceeds the budget and never throws; the
engine-side guard (PlayerMediaSourceFactory) now clamps the session
CONNECTION COUNT first (chunk-clamping pinned the chunk to its 1MB floor
while the window still overcommitted at high counts), keeping the chunk
clamp only for absurd chunk sizes.

Fast start (gate 1): parallelStartupPrefetchUnlocked is set at creation
instead of being held until STATE_READY/first frame - the old lock held
chunk sessions to the reader's own chunk plus the continuation (1-2
connections) for the whole initial buffering phase, stretching cold start
to tens of seconds on throttled lines. STATE_READY/onRenderedFirstFrame
unlock calls remain, harmlessly idempotent.

Capacity: executor 80/160 -> 272/328, shared pool idle 128 -> 512 (2x the
256 cap, aria2-aligned), both dispatchers 128/128 -> 512/512.

Strings: connection-count subtitle updated to the 256-era semantics
(default locale only; translations to follow).
