From 821b62918d1f9b2bbd35d9d4d82aa66ac6238651 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sim <sim@local>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 19:25:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] Parallel engine: hard concurrency gate, fast start,
 adaptive hedge threshold

Connection gate (P1): every window chunk download now acquires a fair
per-instance semaphore sized by parallelConnections, making the setting
the real cap. Without it the fill loop submits depth futures at once and
the executor core - not the setting - decided how many sockets open (the
'set 64, see 80+' overage). Reader continuation stays outside the gate;
cancelled/abandoned futures bail before contending for a permit.

Fast start (gate 2): sessions with >= 32 connections bypass the 1MB
EARNED_PREFETCH re-earn after every open/seek. At throttled per-connection
speeds that gate cost 4-16 seconds of single-connection ramp in exactly
the phase where full concurrency matters most; 1MB chunks meant it was
re-earned one full chunk at a time.

Adaptive hedge (P7): the fixed 256 KB/s (2 Mbps) stall threshold killed
healthy connections whenever the carrier throttled per-connection
throughput below it - each kill costing a fresh TCP slow start and a
TIME_WAIT socket. The threshold now tracks a quarter of the session's
median chunk completion rate (32-sample window, floored at 16 KB/s via
MemoryBudget.hedgeStallThresholdBps), so only genuine stalls (no progress,
or 4x slower than the session median) are restarted. Empty samples fall
back to the legacy CDN/usenet constants, keeping first-chunk behaviour
identical. HEDGE_SAMPLE logs the active threshold (stallBps).

Executor core 80/160 -> 272/328 (deepest legal window 257 + in-flight
headroom + escalations; threads spawn lazily, smaller configs unaffected).
