From 33fd6ce1d835f0e5fd75cc5a468ea6044ca6684a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sim <sim@local>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 04:58:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Parallel engine: gate-based AIMD, bounded cancel drain,
 worker stack trim
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

P2 (429 stability): the connection gate (P1) now carries an AIMD ceiling —
the actual number of concurrent connections — replacing the depth-halving
collapse that pinned throughput to 1-2 connections for minutes. On a 429
episode the ceiling halves (floor: 1/16 of the configured count, min 8, via
MemoryBudget.aimdDecreaseGate); while quiet it recovers additively
(+1/16 per step, <= 16 steps to full). The depth cap keeps its role for
queued work but floors at 1/8 of the window (min 16) and recovers +1/8 per
step instead of +1. The gate moved from per-instance to per-session so
warm-session adoption keeps one stable server-facing budget; config raises
between plays grow it via expandMax. HUD: hudGateCeiling + gate log lines.

P4 (socket warmth): cancelling a chunk mid-body kills its socket because
okhttp only pools fully-read bodies — after a seek, every in-flight chunk
cancelled at once and the refill went through cold re-handshakes. The
cancel/abandon paths now drain a NEARLY-complete body (<= 64 KB, bounded by
MemoryBudget.CANCEL_DRAIN_MAX_BYTES) before teardown, best-effort; larger
remainders are closed since draining would waste exactly the throttled
per-connection bandwidth. Clears any pending cancel interrupt first.

Memory: worker threads get 512 KB stacks (from the 1 MB default) — the 272
lazily-spawned workers never nest deep, and the trim halves both stack
address space and resident overhead. READ_BUFFER_SIZE (64 KB ThreadLocal)
audited and left as is: ~17 MB at full depth is within budget.

P9 (burst-idle, in-memory resolution): with the gate enforcing the real
connection count and P7 no longer killing throttled-healthy connections,
in-flight settles at playback_rate / per_connection_rate (Little's law) —
the equilibrium is stable without a pacing scheduler, and P4 keeps sockets
warm across refill boundaries. The disk-cache variant is explicitly out of
scope per the no-disk-writeback constraint.
