From 4de70b2c7fb00ac68819bdfe46b21404b0f4edbc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sim <sim@local>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 04:58:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Wire playback clients to the shared connection pool +
 AIMD/drain ledger tests
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

P3 (pool): playbackHttpClient and trustAllPlaybackHttpClient never had the
shared 512-idle pool wired in — applyNetworkOptimizations only covers the
createHttpClient derivative, while the chunk engine (OkHttpDataSource in
PlayerMediaSourceFactory) and StreamSpeedTester use playbackHttpClient
DIRECTLY. The whole 256-way window therefore ran on okhttp's default
5-idle pool: every completed chunk could evict a live connection and force
a cold TCP/TLS re-handshake — the single biggest rate destabilizer at high
connection counts. Both builders now set the shared pool explicitly.

Tests: 5 new ledger tests (19 total in MemoryBudgetParallel256Test) —
AIMD gate floor by connection count, halve-but-never-die decrease path,
proportional recovery within 16 steps, depth-cap floor/step, and the
64 KB bounded cancel-drain budget. All 29 tests (19 + 10 legacy) pass
against the real production MemoryBudget source.
